nodecode-verifier is a rewrite of am423's am423/hermes-verifier:

  https://github.com/am423/hermes-verifier
  commit 514ff4a7a937e391fca763ba3c9732103f351531
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Hermes Verifier contributors (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the review loop (runtime.py, delta.py, brief.py, gate.py,
guard.py) as one Common Lisp cell. A finished turn's new messages are read
back out of the session log, the obvious credentials are redacted, the turn is
reviewed on a second model, and the finding it raises stands under this cell's
key as a notice the agent reads in its next request. VERIFIER.md review
priorities are still discovered from the working directory up. The phase gate
survives as a :TOOL hook: an impending destructive or publishing call is shown
to the model, which answers allow or block, and any failure to decide allows
the call.

What was left out, and why: the desktop Verifier page and the dashboard API
(Python and TypeScript over Hermes's own web server, which a cell does not
have); the VERIFIER.yml roster of named verifiers with per-entry model and
tool grants (one verifier and one model here, which is what the roster was for
in most sessions); and the read-only file tools an advisor could be granted
(a cell's verifier reads the transcript it is given, and the priorities file).
The engagement gate that decided whether a turn was worth reviewing at all is
folded into the review itself: one review per turn, skipped when the turn
brought nothing new.
